Requirement Analysis Phase
LegalEase: AI-Powered Legal Document Generator

Requirement Analysis Phase

1. Introduction

The Requirement Analysis Phase is an important stage in the development of the LegalEase project. In this phase, the requirements of the system are identified, analyzed, and documented.

LegalEase is designed to help users create AI-assisted legal document drafts by entering information through a simple interface. The system converts the provided information into a structured document that users can review, edit, and download.

The main goal is to clearly define what the system should do, what users need, and what technologies and resources are required before development begins.


---

2. Project Objective

The main objectives of LegalEase are:

To simplify the process of preparing basic legal document drafts.

To provide an easy-to-use document generation interface.

To collect required information through structured forms.

To use AI to generate well-organized document drafts.

To reduce manual typing and formatting work.

To allow users to review and edit generated documents.

To provide downloadable documents in suitable formats.

To clearly inform users that AI-generated documents require review and are not a substitute for professional legal advice.



---

3. Problem Requirements

The requirement analysis identified the following problems:

1. Users may not know how to structure legal documents.


2. Legal terminology can be difficult for beginners.


3. Manual document preparation takes time.


4. Users may make formatting or content mistakes.


5. Finding appropriate templates can be difficult.


6. Users need a simple method to provide document information.


7. Generated documents should be easy to edit and download.



LegalEase is intended to address these problems through an AI-assisted workflow.


---

4. Functional Requirements

Functional requirements describe what the LegalEase system should do.

4.1 User Interface

The system should provide:

A home page.

Navigation options.

Document selection interface.

Input forms.

Generate button.

Document preview section.

Edit option.

Download option.



---

4.2 Document Selection

The user should be able to select the required document type.

Examples:

Rental agreement draft

Declaration

Authorization letter

Complaint/representation draft

Employment-related document

General agreement

Application or notice draft


The available document types can be expanded as the project develops.


---

4.3 User Information Input

The system should collect the information required for the selected document.

Possible fields include:

Name

Address

Contact details

Date

Purpose

Details of parties involved

Terms and conditions

Duration

Additional information


The form should validate required fields before generation.


---

4.4 AI Document Generation

The system should send the user's information to the AI service.

The AI should:

Understand the user's requirements.

Organize the provided information.

Generate appropriate sections.

Produce clear draft language.

Maintain the information supplied by the user.

Avoid unnecessarily inventing facts.



---

4.5 Document Preview

After generation, the system should display the document to the user.

The user should be able to:

Read the document.

Check entered information.

Identify missing information.

Review the structure.

Make corrections.



---

4.6 Editing

The system should allow users to edit the generated draft.

Users should be able to:

Correct names.

Change dates.

Modify terms.

Add information.

Remove incorrect information.

Regenerate the draft when necessary.



---

4.7 Download

The final draft should be downloadable.

Possible formats include:

PDF

DOCX

TXT



---

4.8 Error Handling

The system should display suitable messages when:

Required information is missing.

Invalid information is entered.

AI generation fails.

Internet/API connection is unavailable.

Document generation encounters an error.



---

5. Non-Functional Requirements

Non-functional requirements describe how well the system should work.

5.1 Usability

The application should be:

Simple

User-friendly

Easy to navigate

Suitable for beginners

Mobile-friendly where possible



---

5.2 Performance

The system should generate documents within a reasonable amount of time and provide feedback while processing.


---

5.3 Security

Since users may enter personal information, the system should:

Protect user data.

Avoid unnecessary storage of sensitive information.

Secure API credentials.

Use secure communication where applicable.

Avoid exposing API keys in frontend code.



---

5.4 Reliability

The application should handle errors properly and prevent the system from crashing when incorrect or incomplete input is provided.


---

5.5 Scalability

The system should be designed so that additional document types and features can be added later.


---

5.6 Maintainability

The source code should be:

Organized

Modular

Easy to understand

Properly documented

Easy to update



---

6. Hardware Requirements

For development:

Computer/Laptop

Minimum 4 GB RAM

Recommended 8 GB RAM or higher

Minimum 10 GB available storage

Keyboard and mouse

Internet connection


For users, a smartphone, tablet, or computer with a modern web browser can be sufficient if the application is deployed as a web application.


---

7. Software Requirements

Possible software requirements include:

Component	Technology

Frontend	HTML, CSS, JavaScript
Backend	Python / Flask or FastAPI
AI	Gemini API or suitable LLM API
Database	SQLite / MySQL
Code Editor	Visual Studio Code
Browser	Chrome / Edge / Firefox
Version Control	Git & GitHub
Document Generation	PDF/DOCX libraries


The exact technology stack can be finalized during the Design Phase.


---

8. AI Requirements

The AI component is the core requirement of LegalEase.

The AI system should be capable of:

Understanding natural-language input.

Generating structured document drafts.

Following document-specific formats.

Using user-provided information correctly.

Producing readable and professional language.

Handling different document categories.

Providing explanations of difficult terms when requested.


AI output should be treated as draft content requiring user review, particularly for legally significant documents.


---

9. Database Requirements

If a database is included, it may store non-sensitive application information such as:

Document type

Generated document metadata

Creation date

User preferences

Document history


Sensitive information should not be stored unnecessarily, and appropriate security measures should be applied.


---

10. User Requirements

The user should be able to perform the following workflow:

Open LegalEase
      ↓
Select Document Type
      ↓
Enter Required Information
      ↓
Validate Information
      ↓
Generate AI Draft
      ↓
Preview Document
      ↓
Edit / Regenerate
      ↓
Download


---

11. Legal and Safety Requirements

Because LegalEase deals with legal documents, the system should include appropriate safeguards.

The application should:

Display a clear disclaimer that generated documents are AI-assisted drafts.

Encourage users to verify important information.

Recommend professional legal assistance for complex or high-risk matters.

Avoid presenting AI-generated content as guaranteed legal advice.

Avoid fabricating legal provisions or citations.

Protect personal information entered by users.

Provide transparent information about AI-generated content.



---

12. Project Constraints

Some possible limitations are:

Dependence on an internet connection for cloud-based AI.

AI API usage may have limits or costs.

AI-generated content may require human verification.

Legal requirements can differ between jurisdictions.

Some complex legal documents cannot safely be handled using a generic template.

Security and privacy must be considered when processing personal information.



---

13. Expected Output

At the end of the process, LegalEase should produce a well-structured legal document draft based on the information entered by the user.

Example:

Input:

> Document Type: Rental Agreement
Tenant: ABC
Owner: XYZ
Duration: 1 Year
Monthly Rent: ₹10,000



Output:

A structured rental-agreement draft containing relevant sections such as:

Parties

Property information

Rental period

Rent

Responsibilities

Terms and conditions

Signature section


The generated document should then be reviewed by the user.


